## Problem

The exact post-merge staging recovery canary for #17173 (run 30224564063, deployed commit `e00283274bd6490f6fedc02fd56f9de92dbe29fd`) found one stale dedicated canary and received `202` for its identity-bound delete, but the async `agent_delete` job exhausted its attempts after 200.9 seconds. The public canary artifact correctly redacts IDs and raw errors, but currently collapses the terminal job to `job_failed`, leaving no sanctioned way to distinguish provider stop, credential revocation, lifecycle ownership, or row-delete failure before a retry.

No replacement agent was created, no chat request ran, and staging health remains green. A blind retry is not acceptable because post-stop failures can be retried after provider-side capacity mutation.

## Scope

Add a workflow-dispatch-only, staging-environment, read-only diagnostic mode to the existing managed dedicated canary workflow. It must:

- accept only an exact `r<run-id>a<attempt>` suffix;
- query the staging database read-only for exactly one matching sandbox and its newest `agent_delete` job;
- emit only a strict allowlisted, privacy-safe diagnostic (statuses, attempts, known result booleans, classified error code, timestamps/durations; no IDs, names, URLs, IPs, database strings, credentials, or raw arbitrary JSON);
- fail closed on zero/multiple targets, malformed rows, or unclassified sensitive text;
- perform zero POST/DELETE/UPDATE/DDL requests or SQL;
- share the canary concurrency group so it cannot race a live canary;
- preserve the normal canary path byte-for-byte unless diagnostic mode is explicitly selected;
- include executable workflow-contract/privacy tests.

## Acceptance

1. Local workflow-contract and sanitizer tests pass.
2. A branch-ref diagnostic dispatch against the failed suffix returns a classified terminal job packet without mutating staging.
3. The packet is sufficient to decide whether a source fix or safe retry is required.
4. Evidence is linked here and in the PR; production is untouched.

Parent epic: #17175

Implement the calendar truth foundation required before family scheduling can make availability claims.

Acceptance criteria:
- preserve connector account and grant identity for calendars, events, sync state, and pruning;
- drain Google calendar/event pages and persist incremental sync tokens per account/calendar;
- recover expired cursors with a full snapshot and apply cancellation tombstones without cross-account deletion;
- preserve attendee RSVP/role, transparency, visibility, and explicit send-update semantics;
- expose complete/partial/unavailable source health so missing data never reads as free time;
- provide privacy-only guest free/busy and deterministic conflict evaluation;
- prove multi-account isolation, partial failure, cursor deltas, 410 recovery, DST/all-day/tentative/declined/private-busy behavior with automated tests.

Evidence must include real provider or sandbox-provider logs and domain artifacts; mocks alone do not close this issue.
